cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  simplexguard: a runtime-assurance (Simplex) architecture benchmark with an
  exact switching condition and its assurance accounting
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-07
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A Simplex runtime-assurance architecture for a discrete-time linear plant
  with an explicitly declared bounded additive disturbance and declared
  polytopic state and input constraints. The maximal robust invariant set of a
  conservative baseline controller is computed by the one-step-set recursion
  and then verified from scratch by an independent exact support-function test
  rather than by sampling. The switching condition is exact: the performance
  input is admitted if and only if the next state lies in the invariant set for
  every admissible disturbance, evaluated by support function on the declared
  polytope, which involves no sampling, no gridding and no tuned threshold. The
  deliverable is the assurance accounting that the switching costs -- switch
  rate, dwell-time distribution per authority, fraction of the episode under
  baseline control, conservatism cost measured as a paired comparison against
  an unguarded run on the same disturbance realisation, constraint-violation
  count and invariant-set exit count -- together with a deliberate
  bound-violation experiment that runs the unchanged guard against a realised
  disturbance larger than the declared one and measures where the guarantee
  fails. A learned switch predictor (an isotonic-calibrated random forest on
  the state) is benchmarked against the exact condition on held-out episodes
  and loses on that condition's own criterion, on both accuracy and per-decision
  latency; the loss is published with its structural reason and without
  retuning. Validation level 3, research grade. Not a verification tool: it
  proves nothing about a plant it was not given. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - runtime-assurance
  - simplex-architecture
  - robust-invariant-set
  - support-function
  - pontryagin-difference
  - constrained-control
  - switch-prediction
  - aerospace-assurance
